# ============================================================
# JAN-SEVA — environment template
# ============================================================
# Copy to `.env.local` for local work. `.env.local` is gitignored and is
# the only one of these files that may ever hold a secret.
#
# Vite exposes ONLY variables prefixed `VITE_` to the browser bundle.
# Anything without that prefix is server-side (the functions in `api/`)
# and must never gain one — a service-role key with a VITE_ prefix is
# published to every visitor on the next deploy.
#
# On Vercel these go in Project Settings → Environment Variables, per
# environment. Nothing below belongs in a committed file except the two
# VITE_ values at the top.

# ============================================================
# CLIENT (bundled into the browser — never secret)
# ============================================================

# Mode — development | demo | production
#
#   development  local work: demo accounts, demo OTP, seeded complaints
#   demo         a shareable build; same behaviour, unmistakably labelled
#   production   the real Gwalior beta: no demo accounts, no Quick Demo,
#                no seeded complaints, no fixed OTP
#
# Absent, a production build defaults to `production`. That is
# deliberate: an unconfigured deploy should lose its demo shortcuts, not
# expose them. `.env.production` currently pins this to `demo` — see the
# note in that file for why, and for the go-live switch.
VITE_APP_MODE=development

# Public origin, for links in SMS and for redirect URLs.
VITE_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:5173

# Supabase's anon key is publishable by design — row-level security is
# what makes it safe (see supabase/migrations/0003_rls.sql), so it
# carries the VITE_ prefix. Not yet used by the client.
# VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
# VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Civic screening feature flags (spec §41)
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# THE DEFAULTS ARE ASYMMETRIC ON PURPOSE. Anything that can act
# AGAINST a citizen is off unless switched on by name; anything that
# only observes is on. A missing variable should cost screening
# coverage, never cost a resident a blocked complaint or a warning SMS.
#
# Observation — on by default, safe to leave unset:
# VITE_AI_SCREENING_ENABLED=true
# VITE_RISK_SCORING_ENABLED=true
# VITE_MODERATION_ENABLED=true
#
# Punitive — OFF by default, WITH ONE EXCEPTION named below. Turn the
# others on deliberately, and only after the false-positive suite has
# been run against real photographs from the city. `npm run selftest`
# covers the rules; it cannot cover your provider's behaviour on your
# citizens' actual photos.
#
#   PRE_SUBMIT_BLOCK refuses a submission before a complaint exists.
#   It is the only path where software alone stops someone filing.
#
#   ⚠ THIS ONE DEFAULTS TO **ON** — it is the exception to the rule
#   above. See config/featureFlags.ts: what makes that defensible is the
#   threshold, not the flag. `meetsBlockThreshold` refuses only when the
#   model is HIGH confident, on a usable image, that civic relevance is
#   VERY_LOW and the photo is a portrait, a screenshot or confidently
#   synthetic. With no provider configured the result is UNAVAILABLE,
#   which allows — so a missing key costs screening, never a complaint.
#
#   Still owed: the false-positive suite against real Gwalior
#   photographs. Until it has run this is a reasoned default, not a
#   measured one. Set the line below to switch blocking off without a
#   code change.
# VITE_PRE_SUBMIT_BLOCK_ENABLED=false
#
#   CITIZEN_WARNING sends an SMS telling someone their complaint was
#   found invalid. Needs an SMS provider, which does not exist yet.
# VITE_CITIZEN_WARNING_ENABLED=false
#
#   ABUSE_RESTRICTION applies cooldowns after CONFIRMED human
#   decisions. Never triggered by AI alone.
# VITE_ABUSE_RESTRICTION_ENABLED=false

# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Evidence capture feature flags (spec §48)
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Same asymmetry: flags that decide whether evidence is CHECKED default
# ON, because a check only observes. Flags that decide whether someone is
# REFUSED default to whatever keeps a real pothole reportable and a real
# repair filable. All of these are ON by default — the lines are here so
# the set is discoverable, not because anything needs setting.
#
# The exception worth understanding is RESOLUTION_CAMERA_ONLY: it
# refuses, and refusing is the point. A department closing complaints
# with gallery photos is the failure the evidence layer exists to
# prevent, and the officer's remedy is immediate — take the photo.
#
# VITE_LIVE_CAMERA_ENABLED=true          # the in-app camera path at all
# VITE_CITIZEN_CAMERA_ONLY=true          # hide the gallery picker in /report
# VITE_RESOLUTION_CAMERA_ONLY=true       # refuse non-live captures as proof
# VITE_IMAGE_REUSE_CHECK_ENABLED=true    # compare against the hash index
# VITE_AI_IMAGE_INTEGRITY_ENABLED=true   # integrity screening on evidence
# VITE_LOCATION_EVIDENCE_ENABLED=true    # record location at capture
# VITE_RESOLUTION_EVIDENCE_REQUIRED=true # no resolution without a photo

# ============================================================
# SERVER ONLY (never prefixed VITE_)
# ============================================================

# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Gemini — civic relevance screening (spec §31, §40)
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Server-side only. A VITE_ prefix on this key publishes it to every
# visitor and it must be rotated immediately if that ever happens.
# GEMINI_API_KEY=
# GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-3.7-flash

#   ⚠ PRIVACY GATE — REQUIRED BEFORE ANY IMAGE IS SENT ⚠
#
# Google's Gemini API terms distinguish Paid from Unpaid Services. On
# UNPAID usage (no billing account linked to the project), submitted
# content IS used to improve Google products and train models. These are
# photographs taken by residents in their own neighbourhoods, often
# containing faces, homes, vehicles and number plates.
#
# The adapter therefore REFUSES to send anything unless this is set to
# `paid`. It is not a switch that makes the feature work — it is an
# operator asserting on the record that billing is linked. Setting it
# falsely does not change Google's terms; it only removes the
# possibility of leaking citizen photos into training data by
# forgetting to configure billing.
#
# Verified 2 Sep 2026 against ai.google.dev/gemini-api/terms and
# /docs/logs-policy. Re-check before go-live: on paid tier, prompts are
# logged ~55 days for abuse detection, and zero-data-retention is
# available separately.
# GEMINI_BILLING_TIER=paid

# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Database — Supabase
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# The service-role key bypasses row-level security. It is the most
# dangerous value in this file. If it ever appears in a browser bundle,
# rotate it immediately; every RLS policy in this project is void while
# it is public.
# SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Identity + sessions
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Used to derive opaque identity references (HMAC-SHA-256) and to salt
# rate-limit subjects. 32+ characters; the server refuses to start
# hashing under a short key rather than producing references that look
# right and protect nothing.
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
#
# CHANGING THIS INVALIDATES EVERY EXISTING IDENTITY REFERENCE — citizens
# would stop matching to their own complaints. Rotate only with a
# migration that re-derives them.
# IDENTITY_SECRET=

# Signs the portal session cookie. Separate from IDENTITY_SECRET so that
# rotating sessions (routine) does not orphan identities (not routine).
# SESSION_SECRET=

# ------------------------------------------------------------
# OTP / SMS provider
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Unset in development, the `console` adapter logs the code instead of
# sending it. That adapter is refused outright in production.
#
#   OTP_PROVIDER=console | msg91 | 2factor | twilio
#
# Every option needs TRAI DLT registration before it can deliver an A2P
# SMS to an Indian number: a registered entity, a registered sender
# header, and each template approved in advance. Days to weeks, and no
# provider exempts anyone from it.
# OTP_PROVIDER=
# OTP_PROVIDER_KEY=
# OTP_TEMPLATE_ID=

# Twilio Verify uses its own three values instead of OTP_PROVIDER_KEY.
# TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
# TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
# TWILIO_VERIFY_SERVICE_SID=

# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Mode, server side
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# The functions cannot read VITE_APP_MODE (it is a build-time client
# value). Set this to `production` on the production deployment so the
# console OTP adapter is refused there.
# APP_MODE=production

# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Geocoding
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Reverse geocoding currently calls OpenStreetMap Nominatim directly
# from the browser with no key. Nominatim's usage policy requires an
# identifying User-Agent and caps requests at 1/sec — neither of which a
# browser client can honour, and it is not licensed for this volume.
# Moving it behind a server function with a real provider is its own
# piece of work.
# GEOCODER_PROVIDER=nominatim
# GEOCODER_API_KEY=
